course: dictionary
lesson: 11415
title: Accusing: saying someone did wrong
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To accuse someone is to say they did something wrong. Accusing is the -ing form of that word. «"You broke the vase!" said Mum, accusing me.» Mum thinks I broke the vase, so accusing means blaming me for it.
explain: Accusing can also describe a look, voice or finger. «Sam pointed an accusing finger at his brother.» Sam is not saying a word, but his finger shows he thinks his brother did something wrong. An accusing look works the same way.
explain: Look at the words near accusing to check its meaning. Did someone break a rule, take something or tell a lie? Those clues show that someone is being blamed. Then try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Accusing means saying that someone has done something wrong.
fact: «Accuse» is the base word. «Accuses», «accused» and «accusing» are its other forms.
fact: As an adjective, «an accusing look» shows you think someone has done wrong, even without words.
fact: «Accusing someone of» something means blaming them for it, as in «accusing him of cheating».
fact: Good friends do not keep accusing others without proof. They say how they feel instead.
exercise: What does accusing mean? | Saying thank you for a gift | *Saying someone did something wrong | Running very fast | Singing loudly in a choir
exercise: «Sam pointed an accusing finger at his brother.» Who is being blamed? | The dog | Sam | *His brother | Nobody
exercise: Which phrase shows blame without any words? | A loud song | A happy smile | *An accusing look | A long walk
test: «She accused him of cheating.» What does accused mean here? | *Said he had done something wrong | Hid his school bag | Gave him a present | Said he was very clever
test: Mia says it is fine to keep accusing people without any proof. Is she right? | Yes, but only if they are your friends | *No, it is better to say how you feel than to blame someone without proof | No, but only on Mondays | Yes, always blame people quickly
exam: «Her accusing voice made everyone look at Tom.» What did her voice show? | She was lost in the park | *She thought Tom had done something wrong | She was singing a happy song | She was fast asleep
